Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text:

```cpp
#include <boost/algorithm/string/case_conv.hpp>
#include <boost/algorithm/string/classification.hpp>
#include <boost/algorithm/string/split.hpp>
#include <boost/algorithm/string/trim.hpp>
#include <boost/lexical_cast.hpp>
#include <unordered_map>

std::string parse_markdown_to_html(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::stringstream html;
    boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(markdown, boost::algorithm::is_space);
    boost::split(
            html,
            markdown,
            boost::is_any_of("[\n\r]"),
            boost::token_compress_on);
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>
        markdownToHTML = {{"[]", "<img>"},
                           {"***", "<p></p>"},
                           {"^^^", "<b></b>"},
                           {"", ""}};
    std::string output;
    for (std::string& token: html) {
        if (token == ".") {
            boost::algorithm::trim_right_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_at_left(" "));
            boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_at_right(" "));
            boost::algorithm::trim_right_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            output.append(token + " ");
        } else if (markdownToHTML.find(token) != markdownToHTML.end()) {
            std::string markdownToken = markdownToHTML[token];
            boost::algorithm::trim_right_if(markdownToken, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(markdownToken, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            output.append(markdownToken + " ");
        } else {
            boost::algorithm::trim_right_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(token, boost::algorithm::is_space);
            output.append(token + " ");
        }
    }
    boost::algorithm::trim_right_if(output, boost::algorithm::is_space);
    boost::algorithm::trim_left_if(output, boost::algorithm::is_space);
    return output;
}
```

This function uses the Boost library to parse the markdown input and generate the corresponding HTML. The `parse_markdown_to_html` function takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown and returns a std::string containing the HTML. The function uses a std::stringstream to store the generated HTML.

The function uses the Boost `algorithm` library to perform various tasks such as trimming whitespace, splitting the markdown based on line breaks, and replacing certain markdown tokens with their corresponding HTML equivalents. The `boost::is_any_of` function is used to split the markdown input based on `[`, `\n`, and `\r`.

The function also uses an unordered map to store the markdown-to-HTML conversions that need to be performed. The keys are the markdown tokens (e.g. "[]", "^^^", etc.) and the values are the corresponding HTML tokens (e.g. "<img>", "<p></p>", "<b></b>", etc.).

Finally, the function iterates over the HTML tokens and performs the necessary replacements and trimming operations.

Here's a function in JavaScript that takes an array of 5 numbers as input and checks if any of the numbers is a valid prime number. If it is, the number is printed to the console:

```javascript
function checkPrime(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    let num = arr[i];
    if (num === 0 || num === 1) {
      console.log("0 and 1 are not prime numbers.");
      break;
    }
    for (let j = 2; j < num; j++) {
      if (num % j == 0) {
        console.log(`${num} is not a prime number.`);
        break;
      }
    }
    if (j == num) {
      console.log(`${num} is a prime number.`);
    }
  }
}

let inputArr = [0, 2, 3, 4, 5];
checkPrime(inputArr);
```
In this function, we first check if the number is either 0 or 1. If it is, we print a message and break from the loop. This is because 0 and 1 are not considered prime numbers.

Next, we iterate through the array using a loop and check if the number is divisible by any number less than it. If it is, we print a message and break from the loop again. Once we reach the end of the loop and the number is not divisible by any number less than it, we print a message to indicate that the number is a prime number.

Note that this function does not check if any of the numbers in the array are valid prime numbers. It checks if the numbers are divisible by any number less than them, and prints a message if they are.

