In TypeScript, there are a few different ways to define a union type:

```typescript
type UnionType = Type1 | Type2;
```

This notation defines a union type of Type1 and Type2.

In order to provide the type system with the confidence that a given value will only have the Type1 type, you can create a discriminated union type, where one of the types includes a common attribute or property that is used to differentiate between the types.

For example:

```typescript
type Foo = {
  foo: "foo";
};

type Bar = {
  bar: "bar";
};

type Union = Foo | Bar;

const unionValue: Union = {
  foo: "foo",
};

// The type system is confident that unionValue will have the Foo type
```

In this case, because the unionValue can only have the Foo type, the type system knows that there is no need to allow the Bar type to be present, and will only allow the Foo type to be present.

